        "original_id": "9a4c8859-5f97-5d2d-9f79-74f079d26466",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Events: Griswold v. Connecticut, Diamond v. Chakrabarty\n- People: Charles Lee Buxton\n- Organizations: Supreme Court of the United States, Yale School of Medicine\n- Concepts: right to privacy, genetically modified organism patents\n\nFacts:\n- Charles Lee Buxton was born on October 14, 1904.\n- Charles Lee Buxton was an American gynecologist and a professor at the Yale School of Medicine.\n- In 1965, the Supreme Court of the United States ruled in Griswold v. Connecticut, 381 U.S. 479, that the Constitution, through the Bill of Rights, implies a fundamental right to privacy.\n- Charles Lee Buxton was an appellant in the US Supreme Court case Griswold v. Connecticut.\n- Charles Lee Buxton died on July 7, 1969.\n- In 1980, the Supreme Court of the United States decided Diamond v. Chakrabarty, 447 U.S. 303, on whether genetically modified organisms can be patented.",
